// **The reed** -- Phase 1 of
// `Reports/aquatic-implementation-plan-2026-09-14.md`, "the margin". The
// first plant in this engine that can stand in water.
//
// **Cut from `grass.ron`, and that is a decision rather than convenience.**
// Grass is the proof that a non-woody plant photosynthesising from
// `MatureBody` works end to end -- `plastochron: 0` everywhere, no `Leaf`
// cell type, income from the tip and the retired blade alike -- and a reed
// is a grass that grew up in a pond. Copying the working economy means the
// only new thing in this file is the water, which is what the phase is for.
// Read `grass.ron`'s header for the economy itself; it applies here
// unchanged and is not repeated.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT MAKES THIS SPECIES AQUATIC: TWO FIELDS, AND THE SECOND WAS NOT IN
// THE PLAN
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **`submerged_shoot: true`** is the one the plan scoped: `plant::growable`
// refuses a shoot every occupied cell, water included, so before this flag a
// plant at a waterline stopped dead at it. Per-species, so all twenty
// shipped plants are a no-op **by construction** -- see
// `SpeciesDef::submerged_shoot` for why that matters more than it sounds.
//
// **`seed_material: "reedseed"`** is the half that was missing, found by
// measurement on 2026-09-18 and recorded in full in `reedseed.ron`. The
// shared `seed.ron` is density 0.6 against water's 1.0, so **a plant seed
// floats**. `the_pond_sediment.ron`'s two herbs, which the plan describes as
// placed "in the sediment", were resting on the water surface 23 rows above
// it for the whole run. So the measurement Phase 1 was scoped from -- dead
// herbs in a pond that holds -- was a seed that never landed, not a shoot
// that could not leave the waterline. Both halves are needed and neither is
// sufficient alone.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SILHOUETTE, WHICH IS THE PART MOST LIKELY TO FAIL
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `Reports/plant-appearance-design.md` §5 is the standing warning this file
// is written against: three architectural levers fired, all measured, all
// counted, and the owner saw no change -- because every plant in the world
// drew from one four-brown palette and one four-green one. A reed that
// borrowed `grassblade` would be a tall grass in a puddle.
//
// So the differences here are the ones that reach *pixels*: its own
// materials, a **glaucous** palette that is a different hue family from both
// existing green sets (`reedstem.ron` has the numbers), and a habit that is
// a bundle of tall near-vertical stems rather than a splayed tuft -- narrow
// branch angle, high heading inertia, strong upward weight. Height is the
// other half of the silhouette and it is set by the bed, below.
//
// **Numbers here are a first pass and are the implementer's.** They are
// swept at landing, rebuilt between every sweep point (`.ron` is
// `include_str!`; identical output across settings means the knob was never
// connected -- `.claude/rules/assets.md`).
(
    name: "reed",
    shoot_material: "reedstem",
    root_material: "reedroot",
    leaf_material: "reedstem",
    // **The file's other load-bearing line.** See the header and
    // `reedseed.ron`.
    seed_material: "reedseed",
    // **The line the phase was scoped as.** See the header and
    // `SpeciesDef::submerged_shoot`.
    submerged_shoot: true,
    // Bands 0..2 of `reedstem`: glaucous, sage, olive-tan. Band 3 is the
    // tawny winter reed bed and is deliberately outside the range, exactly
    // as `grass.ron` holds back its straw -- a stand that starts half-dead
    // reads as broken rather than as varied.
    foliage_bands: (first: 0, count: 3),
    // The same range, for `grass.ron`'s reason: with no separate leaf stage
    // the "bark" band and the "foliage" band index the *same* palette, so
    // keeping them equal is what stops a plant drawing its stem cells out of
    // a different part of the ramp than its tip.
    bark_bands: (first: 0, count: 3),
    // A plant standing in a pond is never short of water. Kept at grass's
    // value rather than zeroed because the field is also the genome's slot-7
    // multiplier, and a species that opts out of it is a species evolution
    // cannot move on that axis.
    stomatal_reserve: 0.2,
    // **An eighth of grass's 18,000, and this was the dominant term in the
    // owner's *"way way less seeds"* (card `…307c1e`, 2026-09-19).** A
    // persistent seed bank is a *pioneer's* strategy -- grass's answer to a
    // bad patch is to wait -- and at that half-life nothing a reed ever set
    // decayed inside a 20,000-frame run, so the standing pile was the whole
    // production history lying on the sediment. Censused before the cut:
    // **550 seeds against 579 reed cells**, half of everything in the pond.
    //
    // Short-lived seed is also what a reed actually has. It spreads by
    // creeping rhizome (`reedroot`), not by banking seed into the mud, so
    // the biology and the picture ask for the same number.
    seed_half_life: 2400.0,
    // 0.6, the only value in the corpus -- taken from `herb`, `scrambler`
    // and `grass` rather than invented here, for the reasons `grass.ron`
    // sets out at this field. Re-derive it from a dispersal sweep before
    // believing any particular value.
    seed_gut_survival: 0.6,
    // Twice grass's 900. A dead sward browns off and is gone; dead reeds
    // stand through a winter, which is most of what a reed bed looks like
    // for half the year.
    remains_half_life: 1800.0,
    // `grass.ron`'s table unchanged -- this is `plant::builtin_fate` written
    // out, and a guard checks the two agree rule by rule.
    fates: [
        (GrowingTip, [
            (when: Node,  becomes: DormantBud, child: Some(GrowingTip), lateral: Some(GrowingTip)),
            (when: Grew,  becomes: MatureBody, child: Some(GrowingTip), lateral: Some(GrowingTip)),
            (when: Stale, becomes: MatureBody),
        ]),
        (RootTip, [
            (when: Grew,  becomes: MatureBody, child: Some(RootTip), lateral: Some(RootTip)),
            (when: Stale, becomes: MatureBody),
        ]),
        (DormantBud, [
            (when: Flush, becomes: GrowingTip),
        ]),
    ],
    cell_types: [
        (Seed, [
            Germinate(
                // **Lower than grass's 0.08.** A reed seed germinates on the
                // bottom of a pond, and even though water does not attenuate
                // light in this engine today (`PIXEL_PHYSICS_WATER_OPACITY`
                // defaults to 0.0, and Phase 3a is what would change that),
                // this must not become the gate that stops the species the
                // day that switch is turned on. Set below the depth the bed
                // can produce rather than at it.
                light_threshold: 0.04,
                // Saturated sediment is the only ground this plant wants, and
                // submerged sediment reads near 1.0. Set well under it rather
                // than near it: the threshold's job here is to be passed.
                soil_water_threshold: 0.10,
                instant: false,
            ),
        ]),
        (GrowingTip, [
            Grow(
                // A reed stem is a stouter thing than a blade of grass, and
                // there are fewer of them.
                cost: 0.15,
                // **Well under grass's 0.4.** A tussock is a splay of
                // tillers; a reed bed is a stand of separate near-parallel
                // stems, and the difference between the two silhouettes is
                // mostly this number and `branch_angle` below.
                branch_chance: [0.05, 0.06],
                continuation_weight: 0.85,
                // Low, like grass: a reed goes up. It does not chase light
                // sideways.
                light_weight: [0.1, 0.1],
                // Under grass's 0.35. A reed sways in wind, but swaying is a
                // `stiffness` question (`reedstem.ron` declines to guess at
                // one yet); this knob moves where the stem is *placed*, and a
                // reed that wanders as it climbs reads as a weed.
                wind_weight: 0.05,
                // **Strongly up, and higher than grass's [0.85, 0.7].** This
                // is a plant whose whole job is to cross 24 rows of water and
                // get its head into the air.
                upward_weight: [0.95, 0.9],
                crowding_weight: 30.0,
                // Fewer than grass's 10: a reed clump is a handful of tall
                // stems, not a dense tuft. Tall and few is the silhouette.
                max_active_tips: 6,
                // **0 = no separate leaf stage**, as grass. The structural
                // line, inherited with the economy.
                plastochron: [0, 0],
                penetration_force: 0.0,
                // **Set from the bed, which is the only honest way to set
                // it.** `the_pond_sediment.ron` puts sediment at y=188 and
                // the waterline at y=164, so a reed has **24 rows of water**
                // to cross before any of it is visible above the surface.
                // The nominal height bound is `(source - yield) /
                // per_cell` = (0.325 - 0.1) / 0.0075 = **30 rows**, which
                // clears the water with six to spare.
                //
                // **That is a path-length bound, not a prediction** --
                // grass's own note -- and a wandering tiller measures well
                // over its nominal while a straight stem measures near it.
                // This species is deliberately straight (`heading_inertia`
                // 0.88 below), so expect measured height near the nominal
                // rather than over it. The measured figure goes in the
                // commit message rather than being guessed at here.
                //
                // **And the plan's deferral still holds.** The elegant change
                // -- charging the lift only above the waterline, since the
                // ceiling is Lockhart's equation paying the gravitational
                // cost of raising water -- is Phase 4 and removes a bound
                // without supplying a replacement. A reed does not need it:
                // it is short by construction, and 30 rows is that
                // construction written down.
                turgor_source: 0.38,
                turgor_yield: 0.1,
                turgor_per_cell: 0.0075,
                turgor_taper: 0.4,
                // **High, and the highest shoot here.** Grass sits at 0.55
                // and wanders, which its own file calls out as the species
                // with the least to hide behind. A reed is a straight line
                // 30 cells long; if it wobbles it is a weed, not a reed.
                heading_inertia: 0.94,
                // **Narrower than grass's 22.** Stems rise nearly parallel
                // out of the crown instead of splaying. A splayed clump
                // reads as a small shrub, which is the failure this file is
                // told to stop and report on.
                branch_angle: [12.0, 10.0],
                internode: [1, 1],
                // Stiffer than grass's [0.9, 0.8] for the same reason
                // `heading_inertia` is higher: a one-cell stem is nothing but
                // its own path, and this one has ~37 cells of it to keep
                // straight.
                //
                // **INERT AT THE SHIPPED DEFAULT, and that is not a bug in
                // this file.** `World::stem_mode` defaults to `StemMode::Off`,
                // whose `forced()` returns `Some(0.0)` -- so the value here is
                // never read unless the selector is moved to `AUTHORED` or
                // `FULL`, or `STEM_STIFFNESS=` is set. Authored anyway, exactly
                // as `grass.ron` authors [0.9, 0.8] it can equally not use,
                // so the species says what it wants the day the switch moves.
                //
                // **It is the knob the reed most needs, and the one verdict
                // against it was taken on a different subject.** Measured
                // 2026-09-19 on this bed, `STEM_STIFFNESS=1.0` against the
                // default: the wandering stems become dead-straight verticals,
                // which is the owner's *"be a little straighter"*. `7d731d54`
                // records him judging this mechanism **"looks identical"** and
                // the switch staying off -- judged on trees, where this file's
                // sibling comment says the wobble is buried under thickening
                // and foliage. A one-cell stem standing in open water has
                // nothing to hide it. Card posted rather than flipped: a
                // global selector is not a species' decision to make.
                stem_stiffness: [0.99, 0.97],
                leaf_cluster: 0,
                juvenile_size: 6,
                juvenile_plastochron: 1.0,
                juvenile_branch: 1.4,
                // Non-zero on the same slots grass varies: a reed must be
                // able to respond to selection through the shared genome
                // rather than a private one.
                genotype_variance: (0.5, 0.0, 0.4, 0.18, 0.7, 0.0, 0.4, 0.5, 0.0, 0.7),
                sympodial: [false],
                tropism: [Orthotropic],
            ),
            // On the tip, because the tip *is* photosynthetic tissue here.
            // Grass's rates and death terms, inherited with its economy --
            // `shade_death` is the live arm on a species with no leaf stage,
            // and `drought_death` is inert for a plant with zero
            // transpirational demand, let alone one standing in a pond.
            Photosynthesize(rate: 0.55, shade_death: 0.004, drought_death: 0.002),
        ]),
        (RootTip, [
            Absorb(rate: 1.6),
            Grow(
                cost: 0.15,
                // A spreading rhizome mat, as grass's.
                branch_chance: [0.45],
                continuation_weight: 0.6,
                light_weight: [0.0],
                wind_weight: 0.0,
                // **Shallower than grass's 0.22, and set from the bed
                // again**: there are only four rows of sediment over an
                // impermeable stone seal, so a rhizome that dives hits stone
                // and stops. At 0.15 it runs sideways through the layer it
                // has, which is both what a rhizome is and what makes
                // `reedroot`'s `reinforces_powder` add up to a held bed.
                upward_weight: [0.15],
                crowding_weight: 0.0,
                max_active_tips: 12,
                plastochron: [0],
                heading_inertia: 0.4,
                // **Must exceed the substrate's `penetration_resistance`,
                // not merely be small** -- `growable` is a strict inequality.
                // Soil is 0.8. Grass learned this the expensive way: its
                // first draft said 0.5 for "fine roots" and the whole
                // species died having never once rooted, with a picture that
                // said "bare soil" and only a counter that said why. 1.0
                // clears soil and stays under sand's 1.4.
                penetration_force: 1.0,
                genotype_variance: (0.0, 0.5, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.4, 0.0, 0.0, 0.25, 0.0),
            ),
        ]),
        (MatureBody, [
            // **`seed_maturity` is SHOOT CELLS, not frames** -- grass's file
            // records a draft at 150 that made reproduction structurally
            // impossible. A reed is a bigger plant than a grass, so this sits
            // above grass's 10; it must still be clearly under the size a
            // stem that has crossed the water actually reaches, which is 30
            // rows before any branching.
            // **A quarter of the allocation and a much larger plant before
            // it may breed at all** (was 0.12 / 24, itself already cut from
            // 0.30 / 16). With `seed_half_life` above, seeds standing at
            // 20,000 frames go **550 -> 27** and the pile on the sediment
            // **367 -> 7**: seed falls from 49% of everything in the pond to
            // 7%. Standing plants 594 -> 44, which is the owner's *"should
            // not crowd so much"* falling out of the same change rather than
            // needing its own.
            //
            // `seed_maturity` is SHOOT CELLS, not frames. 40 is comfortably
            // under the ~37-row stem this species builds *plus* its laterals,
            // so it gates a seedling without gating the species -- checked by
            // measurement rather than by argument, since `grass.ron` records a
            // draft at 150 that made reproduction structurally impossible.
            Reproduce(seed_cost: 0.15, reproductive_allocation: 0.03, seed_maturity: 40),
            StructuralAnchor,
            // **NO `Absorb` here, and this is the one line where copying
            // grass's economy was actively wrong.** `grass.ron` puts
            // `Absorb(1.6)` on `MatureBody` and it costs nothing, because a
            // retired blade stands in air and `absorb_water` only drinks a
            // `Liquid` neighbour. A reed's mature cells stand in the **pond**,
            // so the same line makes every submerged stem cell a straw --
            // about 205 of them at 20,000 frames, drinking the water they are
            // standing in.
            //
            // **Measured, and it is what the owner saw.** With it, the pond
            // stopped being level: reed columns held **11,543 fill against
            // 23,124** in open water and their surface sat ~5 rows lower
            // (y=169.0 against 163.7), while the same bed with the reeds
            // frozen at one cell is level **to the row** -- every column at
            // y=165, min and max identical. The water was being drunk out of
            // the reed bed faster than it could flow back in.
            //
            // A reed drinks through its roots in the sediment like every
            // other plant here; `RootTip` keeps its `Absorb` and the
            // sediment is saturated, so there is nothing to make up.
            
            Photosynthesize(rate: 0.4, shade_death: 0.004, drought_death: 0.002),
        ]),
        (DormantBud, [
            BudBreak(
                cost: 0.15,
                thickening_survival: 0.5,
                acrotony: 0.0,
            ),
            StructuralAnchor,
        ]),
    ],
)
